From: Vadim Khristenko <vadim.khristenko@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] device: keep transport packets that line up with a handshake size

ProcessAWGPacket returned an error when a packet of a handshake message's
size carried no magic header, which is what every transport packet of that
size looks like, and RoutineReceiveIncoming dropped it. The fallback to the
transport path below was meant for this case and was only reached when the
ciphertext happened to match another header.
---
diff --git a/device/device.go b/device/device.go
index fdb8620..f195e40 100644
--- a/device/device.go
+++ b/device/device.go
@@ -882,15 +882,16 @@ func (device *Device) ProcessAWGPacket(size int, packet *[]byte, buffer *[MaxMes
 	// transport size can align with other header types;
 	// making sure we have the right actualMsgType
 	actualMsgType, err := device.getMsgType(packet, junkSize)
-	if err != nil {
-		return 0, fmt.Errorf("get msg type: %w", err)
-	}
-
-	if actualMsgType == expectedMsgType {
+	if err == nil && actualMsgType == expectedMsgType {
 		*packet = (*packet)[junkSize:]
 		return actualMsgType, nil
 	}
 
+	// A transport packet whose size lines up with a handshake message has
+	// ciphertext where that message's header would be, which almost never
+	// matches any magic header. Returning that error dropped the packet, so
+	// every transport packet of exactly those sizes was lost; it goes to the
+	// transport path instead, as it does in 1.0 and from 2.0 on.
 	device.log.Verbosef("awg: transport packet lined up with another msg type")
 
 	msgType, err := device.handleTransport(size, packet, buffer)
